    "result_text": "All tests pass and the build is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Summary\n\nAdded a structured address form (`addressLine1` + optional `addressLine2`) alongside the existing flat `address`, preferring the structured fields when present while staying backward-compatible.\n\n**Core logic \u2014 `AddressNormalizer`** (the existing canonical address helper):\n- `compose(addressLine1, addressLine2)` \u2014 the normalized `addressLine1`, with a single space and the normalized `addressLine2` appended only when a second line is present.\n- `resolve(addressLine1, addressLine2, flatAddress)` \u2014 the single decision point for the \"prefer structured, else flat\" policy. Returns a `Normalized` record (normalized lines + composed flat `address`). This is reused by both the create and update paths so the rule lives in one place.\n\n**Pipeline steps:**\n- `NormalizeAddress` (POST, runs first) now resolves via `AddressNormalizer.resolve`, republishing normalized `addressLine1`/`addressLine2` and the composed `address`.\n- `ApplyOwner` (PUT) uses the same `resolve`, so update now normalizes/composes too (the spec's intended behaviour change; it previously stored the raw address).\n- `ValidateOwnerFields` needed no change: since `NormalizeAddress` composes the structured form into `address` before it runs, the existing \"address present, plus city\" check correctly accepts either form and rejects an owner with neither.\n\n**Model / persistence / contract:**\n- `Owner` entity gained `addressLine1` / `addressLine2` columns + accessors.\n- Added `address_line1` / `address_line2` to all four DB schemas (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres).\n- `openapi.yml` `OwnerFields` gained `addressLine1` / `addressLine2`; `address` was dropped from the `required` list (it's now conditionally required via either form), with descriptions documenting the composition. `OwnerMapper` maps the new fields automatically by name.\n\nThe `household hash`, `postcode validation` and `locality` read `postcode` and `city`, which are top-level fields identical in both forms, so they already \"use the structured fields with flat fallback\" \u2014 and anything reading `owner.getAddress()` now transparently gets the composed structured value.\n\n**Tests:** `AcceptanceTest` (both the structured-address and flat-address backward-compat cases) and the full suite pass \u2014 `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`.",
